---

# Internal Flow of `println(obj)`

This is the real internal working.

Suppose:

```java
System.out.println(s);
```

## Step 1

`System.out` is a `PrintStream` object.

---

## Step 2

`println(Object x)` method gets called.

Inside `PrintStream`:

```java
public void println(Object x) {
    String s = String.valueOf(x);
    synchronized(this) {
        print(s);
        newLine();
    }
}
```

---

# Step 3 — `String.valueOf(x)`

This method checks:

```java
if (obj == null)
    return "null";
else
    return obj.toString();
```

So Java safely calls `toString()`.

---

# Step 4 — Your Overridden Method Runs

If you overrode `toString()`:

```java
public String toString() {
    return "Roll = " + roll;
}
```

that result is returned.

---

# Step 5 — Printing Happens

Finally:

```java
print(s)
```

prints the generated string to console.

---

# Complete Internal Chain

```text
System.out.println(student)

↓
println(Object obj)

↓
String.valueOf(obj)

↓
obj.toString()

↓
Returns String

↓
Console Output
```

